WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:06.040
So stepping up the complexity a tiny bit here, we'll talk about creating masked materials

2
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:07.320
with some nodes.

3
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:11.199
So we want to cover the process of how you might create a masked material, we'll talk

4
00:00:11.199 --> 00:00:16.379
about using minus nodes and clamps, and we'll also use the Lerp node to linear

5
00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:20.640
interpolate between two different channels of the material that we want.

6
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.899
We can talk about masking textures for materials using one texture, and we'll also

7
00:00:24.899 --> 00:00:30.899
talk about using the RBG RGB channels to almost get a more performant texture based

8
00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:33.700
on a specific channel rather than the overall texture.

9
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.100
So let's jump into this.

10
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:39.140
So we've got the process, there are two ways that can be achieved when we're talking

11
00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:40.979
about masked materials.

12
00:00:40.979 --> 00:00:46.259
So with an outside alpha embedded in a texture, or you can do it in a standalone texture

13
00:00:46.259 --> 00:00:49.539
that has an alpha data plugged into opacity.

14
00:00:49.539 --> 00:00:54.979
You can also do this through the color channels, which we were talking about with the RGB

15
00:00:54.979 --> 00:00:55.979
material setup.

16
00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:02.500
When you create a texture sample, you'll have an alpha pin on the texture sample,

17
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:06.939
which is the main output, and then it's the RGB, and then you've got an A.

18
00:01:06.939 --> 00:01:12.219
So anything imported with some kind of opacity, a 32-bit alpha channel, you can use that

19
00:01:12.219 --> 00:01:15.079
alpha to plug into an opacity of the material.

20
00:01:15.079 --> 00:01:18.900
You can even just pull in a specific channel, whether it's the green channel, the red

21
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:23.859
channel, the blue channel, to have an alphered material as well, depends on what you

22
00:01:23.859 --> 00:01:28.420
packed into that texture sheet to begin with, but suffice to say there's a number of ways

23
00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:32.219
that we can achieve creating a masked material.

24
00:01:32.219 --> 00:01:35.140
And it's up to your kind of preference and also just workflow of how you're trying

25
00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:39.109
to pack textures together on the approach that you want.

26
00:01:39.109 --> 00:01:45.269
When we're looking at a material setup that we want for thinking about masked materials,

27
00:01:45.269 --> 00:01:48.469
we'll use the minus nodes and some clamps here as well.

28
00:01:48.469 --> 00:01:54.230
So we'll create or use the black and white texture, which we provided in the project,

29
00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:56.269
and you can create a texture sample.

30
00:01:56.269 --> 00:02:00.469
So a nice little shortcut here is if you do use the texture that was given in the

31
00:02:00.469 --> 00:02:05.109
project or anything that's already in your content browser, you can have that selected

32
00:02:05.109 --> 00:02:07.329
in your content browser.

33
00:02:07.329 --> 00:02:09.289
Don't double click it, you can just select it.

34
00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:13.969
You can come into the material and if you press T on your keyboard and left click,

35
00:02:13.969 --> 00:02:19.289
it will first of all create a texture sample, but it'll also automatically apply the texture

36
00:02:19.289 --> 00:02:21.849
that you had selected in your content browser.

37
00:02:21.849 --> 00:02:24.210
So it's just a nice quicker way of working.

38
00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:28.930
Again, you could always just press T or just right click, add a texture sample and

39
00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:31.650
then select the texture after the effect.

40
00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:36.659
It doesn't matter either way, but it's just a little bit more streamlined way of working.

41
00:02:36.659 --> 00:02:40.539
Next we want to create a one minus if we need to flip the masking effect.

42
00:02:40.539 --> 00:02:44.379
So this is our one minus and it'll essentially just flip to the opposite.

43
00:02:44.379 --> 00:02:50.340
So if you've got Alfred on the outside and the solid colors on the inside, the one minus

44
00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:54.740
will then flip it to translucent outside and opaque on the inside.

45
00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:58.860
So it'll just basically flip the effect that you're going for, which you can see

46
00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.180
this one minus doing there.

47
00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:05.460
So instead of importing a brand new texture that does the same thing, but in the opposite

48
00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:10.580
way, we can actually just dial that in based on the material, which reduces the amount

49
00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:16.219
of texture samples you might need in your project, which is really useful for having that flexibility.

50
00:03:16.219 --> 00:03:20.259
So we've done this to make some kind of gold trim on the material.

51
00:03:20.259 --> 00:03:26.180
Next we'll create a clamp to keep things in range if parameters are introduced for control.

52
00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:30.539
So if someone programs into your shader, they will see that they have a clamp to keep things

53
00:03:30.539 --> 00:03:31.539
in a nice certain range.

54
00:03:31.539 --> 00:03:37.259
So that's just more of a quality control type of clamp that we've put in there to make

55
00:03:37.259 --> 00:03:39.620
sure we're getting the desired effect.

56
00:03:39.659 --> 00:03:42.860
We can also use lerp nodes, which is a linear interpolate.

57
00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:45.740
So you can just right click, add lerp.

58
00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:49.539
You'll likely see that it's called linear interpolate as well.

59
00:03:49.539 --> 00:03:55.379
And so we'll create a lerp node and run the one minus into the alpha input of the lerp.

60
00:03:55.379 --> 00:03:58.780
And then we want to duplicate that process for the material texture.

61
00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:04.020
So when we talk about duplicating the process, you can actually select these nodes, control

62
00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:09.300
C to copy, control V to paste and just work a little bit more efficiently there.

63
00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:13.300
So you just want to notice that there's not a metal texture for the wood, so we can plug

64
00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:15.539
in a constant into the lerp.

65
00:04:15.539 --> 00:04:18.660
So this is the setup that we're going for here.

66
00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:22.579
You can see that we've got that constant going into the A, we've got the texture

67
00:04:22.579 --> 00:04:28.459
sample going into the B and the alpha is actually based on this texture sample here.

68
00:04:28.459 --> 00:04:32.620
And then when we talk about masking textures for materials using one texture, you can

69
00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:37.139
actually see that we've got, if you go into your texture and you click through the

70
00:04:37.139 --> 00:04:42.220
RGB channels, you'll see there's different information packed into the various channels.

71
00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:47.180
And we can actually just pull off, say the green pin or the red pin in this instance

72
00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:50.180
to get specific information from that channel.

73
00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:57.459
So if in your external app, you've either painted or included some information onto

74
00:04:57.459 --> 00:05:05.139
a specific channel instead of just using the main RGB channel, if you're layering stuff,

75
00:05:05.139 --> 00:05:10.379
for example, in Photoshop, you can just pull off that information in the RGB channel or

76
00:05:10.379 --> 00:05:13.379
even the alpha channel and use that.

77
00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:15.540
And so we call that texture packing.

78
00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:21.180
You can pack multiple textures into one texture when importing it into Unreal.

79
00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:24.819
And it's just a slightly more efficient way to work, especially if you're trying to keep

80
00:05:24.819 --> 00:05:29.379
the texture sample instructions down to a minimum.

81
00:05:29.379 --> 00:05:32.379
So go ahead and follow through those steps.

82
00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:37.420
You can create a mask material, play around with the type of masking approach that you'd like.

83
00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:42.180
Again, we kind of noted two or three different ways that you could create a mask material

84
00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:43.939
based on the texture samples.

85
00:05:43.939 --> 00:05:48.660
And again, it's going to be project specific, depending on what you're trying to achieve.

86
00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:51.939
But hopefully that gives a good overview to the mask materials.

87
00:05:51.939 --> 00:05:56.819
And also just to add at the end of this that we want to credit to Frick for the

88
00:05:56.819 --> 00:05:59.579
scene shown in the RGB channel.

89
00:05:59.579 --> 00:06:03.779
And then we've also got a documentation on how to mask materials, if you'd like to dig

90
00:06:03.779 --> 00:06:05.779
into that topic further.
